My TODO list for the next versions:

  - Better safeguards against file upload interruptions.
    
    Currently, if the program is interrupted while uploading, the device is
    still expecting the specified amount of data to come in. There's no way to
    tell the program to abort the upload process, so any further commands will
    get eaten up as garbage data.

    A possible solution: A lock-file that contains information on the most
    recent upload, as well as its file length. During normal procedure, the
    lock file will be removed, but if the program is interrupted, and it sees
    an existing lock-file on startup, it will then use the information to clear
    out the device's buffer by padding it out until it's satisfied, then
    continue with normal procedures.

    Another solution, which could complement the above idea, would be an IPC
    signal handler. If the user aborts by sending SIGINT (Ctrl-C on UNIX), the 
    program should gracefully handle the situation somehow.

  - ROM verification after upload.
    
    When a ROM is uploaded, it should try and grab the data back from the
    device again, and store it in memory, so a data comparison can be done.
    If there's a mismatch, then report an error.

    The device firmware has no way of self-verifying its own data, so this
    could have some issues. For example, how would we know that the device 
    didn't drop USB packets when sending *back* the data to the PC? The data
    internally might be good, but we won't know for sure. It's a bit messy,
    since the firmware is very simplistic. (maybe a feature idea for a future
    sd2snes firmware patch?)

  - Implement "low-performance mode"

    If the host computer is too slow, and the user experiences a higher 
    frequency of dropped USB packets, the program should provide the user with
    a "low-performance mode", where the upload speed is slowed down and with a
    slight delay between transmissions, in order to make sure the host doesn't
    send data too fast for its own good. So far, this behavior has only been 
    observed on a Raspberry Pi 3, but most modern computers won't have this 
    issue at all, but it will still be a nice thing to have for those with
    slower PCs.